{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LCjQEZXCFyAPcXCzQEzAMJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Per-DOF advantage exists but attenuated relative to synthetic\n2\t  MVP. Story pivots from \"per-DOF is the key innovation\" to \"structured uncertainty\n3\t  conditioning (including per-DOF) significantly improves inference-time correction.\"\n4\t  Ablation decomposition: CRM-alone (A3) provides modest correction (+3.5% over base);\n5\t  adding any calibrated uncertainty signal (scalar, A4) gives a substantial additional\n6\t  jump (+2.6%); upgrading to per-DOF gives a further incremental +2.1%. The combined\n7\t  uncertainty contribution (+4.7%) exceeds the residual-only contribution (+3.5%),\n8\t  justifying the claim that calibrated uncertainty features are the primary driver\n9\t  — but the per-DOF vs scalar margin is thin enough that \"per-DOF is additionally\n10\t  beneficial\" is more honest than \"per-DOF is essential.\" COC is a positive but weak\n11\t  self-referential signal (ρ~0.38); reposition as \"promising\" rather than \"reliable,\"\n12\t  with episode-level ACI as the pragmatic recommendation and COC as future work. SRPO\n13\t  stacking gain is real (~2.9%) but near the kill threshold — frame as \"complementary\n14\t  in principle, with modest empirical stacking gains.\" Per-perturbation breakdown\n15\t  is the honesty centerpiece: 4/7 dimensions show significant gain (camera, noise,\n16\t  robot-state, lighting); layout and language see negligible improvement, honestly\n17\t  attributed to synthetic augmentation''s inability to cover these semantic dimensions.\n18\t  Still viable for CoRL poster with softened claims.\n19\t\n20\t  '\n21\timagined_experiments:\n22\t- name: ACR-VLA Full Pipeline (SmolVLA base)\n23\t  plan_id: plan_001\n24\t  description: 'Full ACR-VLA pipeline: CGS (K=10, Ledoit-Wolf) + ACI/COC + CRM (131K\n25\t    params). CRM trained on LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation (camera jitter,\n26\t    Gaussian noise, robot-state perturbation). Evaluated on LIBERO nominal (10 tasks)\n27\t    and LIBERO-Plus (7 perturbation dimensions × 10 tasks).\n28\t\n29\t    '\n30\t  expected_results:\n31\t    libero_nominal_success_rate: 92.6\n32\t    libero_nominal_base: 94.8\n33\t    libero_nominal_drop: 2.2\n34\t    libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 70.5\n35\t    libero_plus_base: 62.3\n36\t    libero_plus_gain_abs: 8.2\n37\t    libero_plus_camera_shift: 70.8\n38\t    libero_plus_sensor_noise: 66.4\n39\t    libero_plus_robot_state: 65.3\n40\t    libero_plus_lighting: 71.2\n41\t    libero_plus_background: 71.5\n42\t    libero_plus_layout: 73.0\n43\t    libero_plus_language: 72.4\n44\t    crm_inference_overhead_ms: 3.2\n45\t    cgs_k10_sampling_time_ms: 118\n46\t    total_inference_time_per_step_ms: 134\n47\t  result_rationale: 'Flow-matching samples from real VLA have covariance structure\n48\t    but noisier than synthetic MVP: K=10 Ledoit-Wolf estimates are adequate but not\n49\t    tight, introducing ~15-20% feature quality degradation vs oracle covariance. CRM\n50\t    still learns meaningful correction patterns because per-DOF std and conformal\n51\t    radii carry genuine distributional information even with shrunk covariance. The\n52\t    8.2% aggregate gain comes primarily from camera/noise/robot-state dimensions where\n53\t    synthetic augmentation provides good calibration-set coverage — CRM learns to\n54\t    suppress high-uncertainty DOFs (typically wrist rotation and z-translation under\n55\t    camera shift) while preserving confident DOFs. The 2.2% nominal drop is CRM occasionally\n56\t    over-correcting in-distribution actions where CGS correctly reports low uncertainty\n57\t    but CRM''s learned residual still nudges slightly; this is the cost of the correction\n58\t    module''s expressivity. Gain is real but not as dramatic as the MVP''s ~27% per-DOF\n59\t    advantage because real VLA action distributions are higher-dimensional (7-DOF\n60\t    chunks vs 3D), multimodal, and the covariance structure is harder to estimate\n61\t    reliably with K=10.\n62\t\n63\t    '\n64\t  tables:\n65\t  - name: 'Table 1: LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus Success Rates'\n66\t    content: '| Method       | LIBERO (nominal) | LIBERO-Plus (agg.) | Δ Plus |\n67\t\n68\t      |-------------|------------------|-------------------|--------|\n69\t\n70\t      | SmolVLA     | 94.8             | 62.3              | —      |\n71\t\n72\t      | ACR-VLA     | 92.6             | 70.5              | +8.2   |\n73\t\n74\t      '\n75\t  - name: 'Table 1 (extended): Per-Perturbation Breakdown'\n76\t    content: '| Perturbation  | SmolVLA | ACR-VLA | Δ     | Significant? |\n77\t\n78\t      |--------------|---------|---------|-------|-------------|\n79\t\n80\t      | camera_shift | 58.2    | 70.8    | +12.6 | ✓           |\n81\t\n82\t      | sensor_noise | 55.6    | 66.4    | +10.8 | ✓           |\n83\t\n84\t      | robot_state  | 56.8    | 65.3    | +8.5  | ✓           |\n85\t\n86\t      | lighting     | 65.4    | 71.2    | +5.8  | ✓           |\n87\t\n88\t      | background   | 68.3    | 71.5    | +3.2  | ✗           |\n89\t\n90\t      | layout       | 72.1    | 73.0    | +0.9  | ✗           |\n91\t\n92\t      | language     | 71.5    | 72.4    | +0.9  | ✗           |\n93\t\n94\t      '\n95\t  figures_description:\n96\t  - 'Fig 2a: Grouped bar chart — SmolVLA vs ACR-VLA success rate across 7 perturbation\n97\t    dimensions + nominal, sorted by ACR-VLA gain. Clear visual separation on camera/noise/robot-state,\n98\t    overlapping bars on layout/language.'\n99\t  - 'Fig 2b: Per-task heatmap (10 tasks × 7 perturbations) showing success rate delta.\n100\t    Reveals that gains are concentrated in manipulation-heavy tasks (e.g., pick-and-place,\n101\t    drawer) rather than navigation tasks.'\n102\t  analysis: 'The 8.2% aggregate gain is solidly within the median range [6, 11]. The\n103\t    per-perturbation structure is informative: the 4 dimensions with significant gain\n104\t    (camera, noise, robot-state, lighting) are precisely those covered by synthetic\n105\t    augmentation, confirming that CGS calibration quality is the bottleneck. Layout\n106\t    and language perturbations involve semantic shifts that synthetic augmentation\n107\t    cannot cover — CGS features for these are essentially noise, and CRM correctly\n108\t    learns to make near-zero corrections. The 2.2% nominal drop is acceptable for\n109\t    CoRL but should be discussed honestly.\n110\t\n111\t    '\n112\t- name: A3 Ablation — CRM without Conformal Features\n113\t  plan_id: plan_002\n114\t  description: 'CRM receives only observation embeddings (from VLA encoder) as input,\n115\t    no CGS-derived uncertainty features. Equivalent to an A2C2-style generic learned\n116\t    residual policy. Same CRM architecture (131K params), same training data and procedure\n117\t    as full ACR-VLA.\n118\t\n119\t    '\n120\t  expected_results:\n121\t    libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 65.8\n122\t    libero_plus_gain_over_base: 3.5\n123\t    a3_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 4.7\n124\t    libero_nominal_success_rate: 93.8\n125\t    libero_plus_camera_shift: 63.1\n126\t    libero_plus_sensor_noise: 60.2\n127\t    libero_plus_robot_state: 60.9\n128\t    libero_plus_lighting: 67.8\n129\t    libero_plus_background: 69.5\n130\t    libero_plus_layout: 72.6\n131\t    libero_plus_language: 71.8\n132\t  result_rationale: 'Without uncertainty features, CRM can only learn a fixed residual\n133\t    correction pattern conditioned on visual observations. It captures some systematic\n134\t    biases (e.g., consistent z-offset under camera shift) but cannot adapt correction\n135\t    magnitude or direction to per-step uncertainty. The 3.5% gain over base reflects\n136\t    that observation-conditioned residuals capture low-hanging fruit — average correction\n137\t    vectors that are right more often than wrong. But the lack of dimension-selective\n138\t    information means CRM applies similar corrections to confident and uncertain DOFs\n139\t    alike, sometimes degrading already-accurate dimensions. The 4.7% gap to full ACR-VLA\n140\t    (within [3,7]) confirms that conformal features provide substantial value — almost\n141\t    as much as the residual module itself — but the gap isn''t overwhelming, consistent\n142\t    with the median tier''s \"per-DOF advantage exists but attenuated\" characterization.\n143\t    The kill criterion (gap < 2%) is clearly survived; Claim 1 holds with the softened\n144\t    framing.\n145\t\n146\t    '\n147\t  tables:\n148\t  - name: Ablation Table Row (A3)\n149\t    content: '| Variant              | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Base | Δ vs Full ACR-VLA\n150\t      |\n151\t\n152\t      |---------------------|-------------|-----------|-------------------|\n153\t\n154\t      | SmolVLA (base)      | 62.3        | —         | -8.2              |\n155\t\n156\t      | A3: CRM, no unc.   | 65.8        | +3.5      | -4.7              |\n157\t\n158\t      | Full ACR-VLA        | 70.5        | +8.2      | —                 |\n159\t\n160\t      '\n161\t  figures_description:\n162\t  - 'Part of Fig 3 (ablation bar chart): A3 bar sits between base and full ACR-VLA,\n163\t    visually demonstrating that conformal features account for roughly 57% of the\n164\t    total gain (4.7/8.2).'\n165\t  analysis: 'A3 gap of 4.7% clearly exceeds the 2% kill threshold. Claim 1 survives\n166\t    but the narrative must acknowledge that observation-conditioned residuals alone\n167\t    provide non-trivial value (+3.5%). The story should frame this as: \"CRM provides\n168\t    a correction mechanism; conformal features provide the information that makes\n169\t    the correction dimension-selective and magnitude-appropriate.\"\n170\t\n171\t    '\n172\t- name: A4 Ablation — Scalar Uncertainty Features\n173\t  plan_id: plan_003\n174\t  description: 'CRM receives observation embeddings + scalar uncertainty features\n175\t    (Euclidean distance from mean to nearest calibration point, analogous to ReconVLA''s\n176\t    approach) instead of 45D per-DOF features. Scalar features: 1D distance + 1D log-likelihood\n177\t    + 1D normalized rank = 3D total.\n178\t\n179\t    '\n180\t  expected_results:\n181\t    libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 68.4\n182\t    libero_plus_gain_over_base: 6.1\n183\t    a4_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 2.1\n184\t    a4_gap_to_a3: 2.6\n185\t    libero_nominal_success_rate: 93.2\n186\t    libero_plus_camera_shift: 67.5\n187\t    libero_plus_sensor_noise: 63.8\n188\t    libero_plus_robot_state: 63.0\n189\t    libero_plus_lighting: 69.8\n190\t    libero_plus_background: 70.4\n191\t    libero_plus_layout: 72.8\n192\t    libero_plus_language: 72.1\n193\t  result_rationale: 'Scalar uncertainty gives CRM a \"how uncertain overall\" signal,\n194\t    enabling it to modulate correction magnitude globally — correct more aggressively\n195\t    when scalar uncertainty is high, less when low. This captures the bulk of the\n196\t    uncertainty-conditioning benefit (+2.6% over A3), because the dominant failure\n197\t    mode under distribution shift is \"everything is somewhat off\" rather than \"one\n198\t    specific DOF is wrong.\" The remaining per-DOF advantage (+2.1%) comes from the\n199\t    minority of cases where uncertainty is truly dimension-selective: e.g., camera\n200\t    shift primarily affecting x/y while z remains well-estimated, or robot-state perturbation\n201\t    affecting specific joints. In these cases, per-DOF features allow CRM to correct\n202\t    the problematic dimensions without disturbing the accurate ones. The 2.1% gap\n203\t    (within [1,3]) is modest but positive, supporting the softened claim that \"per-DOF\n204\t    provides incremental gains over scalar.\" Most of the information-theoretic content\n205\t    of the 45D features is captured by the scalar summary — the per-DOF structure\n206\t    adds refinement rather than fundamentally new capability.\n207\t\n208\t    '\n209\t  tables:\n210\t  - name: Ablation Table Row (A4)\n211\t    content: '| Variant              | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Base | Δ vs A3 | Δ vs Full\n212\t      |\n213\t\n214\t      |---------------------|-------------|-----------|---------|-----------|\n215\t\n216\t      | SmolVLA (base)      | 62.3        | —         | —       | -8.2      |\n217\t\n218\t      | A3: CRM, no unc.   | 65.8        | +3.5      | —       | -4.7      |\n219\t\n220\t      | A4: CRM + scalar   | 68.4        | +6.1      | +2.6    | -2.1      |\n221\t\n222\t      | Full ACR-VLA        | 70.5        | +8.2      | +4.7    | —         |\n223\t\n224\t      '\n225\t  figures_description:\n226\t  - 'Part of Fig 3 (ablation bar chart): A4 bar closer to full ACR-VLA than to A3,\n227\t    visually showing that most of the ''uncertainty signal'' value is captured by\n228\t    scalar, with per-DOF providing incremental lift.'\n229\t  - 'Fig 3 inset: Stacked contribution decomposition — CRM alone (3.5%), +scalar unc.\n230\t    (2.6%), +per-DOF upgrade (2.1%). Shows ''any uncertainty >> none'' while per-DOF\n231\t    is beneficial but not dominant.'\n232\t  analysis: 'The A4 gap of 2.1% is within [1,3] as expected for median tier. The critical\n233\t    insight for the paper: the decomposition base→A3→A4→Full shows gains of +3.5/+2.6/+2.1.\n234\t    The transition from \"no uncertainty\" to \"any uncertainty\" (+2.6) is larger than\n235\t    \"scalar to per-DOF\" (+2.1), but per-DOF still provides meaningful additional value.\n236\t    The narrative should emphasize that all three components contribute and per-DOF\n237\t    is the final piece that enables truly dimension-selective correction.\n238\t\n239\t    '\n240\t- name: ACR-VLA Stacked on SRPO\n241\t  plan_id: plan_004\n242\t  description: 'Train new CRM on SRPO-finetuned VLA checkpoint. New CGS calibration\n243\t    on SRPO samples (K=10). SRPO already partially addresses robustness via training-time\n244\t    optimization — question is whether inference-time conformal correction provides\n245\t    orthogonal value.\n246\t\n247\t    '\n248\t  expected_results:\n249\t    srpo_base_libero_plus: 82.1\n250\t    srpo_plus_acr_vla_libero_plus: 85.0\n251\t    srpo_stacking_gain_abs: 2.9\n252\t    srpo_plus_acr_vla_libero_nominal: 93.1\n253\t    srpo_base_libero_nominal: 95.6\n254\t    srpo_plus_acr_vla_camera: 87.2\n255\t    srpo_plus_acr_vla_noise: 84.6\n256\t    srpo_plus_acr_vla_robot_state: 83.8\n257\t    srpo_plus_acr_vla_lighting: 85.4\n258\t    srpo_plus_acr_vla_background: 84.8\n259\t    srpo_plus_acr_vla_layout: 83.5\n260\t    srpo_plus_acr_vla_language: 83.2\n261\t    crm_on_srpo_feature_quality_degradation_pct: 22\n262\t  result_rationale: 'SRPO''s training-time optimization partially collapses the flow-matching\n263\t    sample diversity — the model is more deterministic post-finetuning, reducing the\n264\t    information content of K=10 samples. CGS features extracted from SRPO samples\n265\t    have ~22% lower variance compared to base VLA samples, meaning the uncertainty\n266\t    signal is attenuated. CRM still finds residual patterns to correct but the margin\n267\t    is narrower because SRPO has already addressed many of the systematic biases that\n268\t    CRM would otherwise correct. The 2.9% stacking gain (within [2,4]) comes from:\n269\t    (1) dimensions where SRPO''s training augmentation didn''t cover the specific\n270\t    perturbation pattern (e.g., specific camera angle combinations), and (2) high-uncertainty\n271\t    steps where SRPO''s flow-matching still produces diffuse samples and CRM''s conformal\n272\t    features provide useful correction signal. The gain is near but above the 2% kill\n273\t    threshold, supporting the claim \"complementary in principle, with modest empirical\n274\t    stacking gains.\" The 85.0% stacked result (within [84,86]) would be a new LIBERO-Plus\n275\t    SOTA.\n276\t\n277\t    '\n278\t  tables:\n279\t  - name: 'Table 2: SRPO Stacking Results'\n280\t    content: '| Method              | LIBERO (nom.) | LIBERO-Plus | Δ Plus  |\n281\t\n282\t      |--------------------|---------------|-------------|---------|\n283\t\n284\t      | SmolVLA (base)     | 94.8          | 62.3        | —       |\n285\t\n286\t      | ACR-VLA            | 92.6          | 70.5        | +8.2    |\n287\t\n288\t      | SRPO               | 95.6          | 82.1        | +19.8   |\n289\t\n290\t      | SRPO + ACR-VLA     | 93.1          | 85.0        | +22.7   |\n291\t\n292\t      | Stacking gain      | -2.5          | +2.9        |         |\n293\t\n294\t      '\n295\t  figures_description:\n296\t  - 'Fig 4: Bar chart comparing SRPO vs SRPO+ACR-VLA across perturbation dimensions.\n297\t    Gains are small but consistent. Annotation highlighting 2.9% aggregate gain with\n298\t    95% CI from 5 seeds.'\n299\t  - 'Fig 4 inset: CGS feature variance comparison — base VLA vs SRPO samples. Shows\n300\t    ~22% variance reduction, explaining attenuated per-DOF signal.'\n301\t  analysis: 'The 2.9% stacking gain survives the kill criterion (>2%) but barely.\n302\t    The paper should frame this cautiously: \"ACR-VLA provides orthogonal value even\n303\t    atop training-time optimization, though the margin is modest — consistent with\n304\t    SRPO having already resolved many of the uncertainty patterns that ACR-VLA targets.\"\n305\t    The 85.0% SOTA claim is legitimate but should acknowledge the narrow margin. Nominal\n306\t    drop of 2.5% on SRPO is slightly higher than on base VLA, possibly because SRPO''s\n307\t    actions are more precise and CRM''s residuals are more likely to degrade already-good\n308\t    actions.\n309\t\n310\t    '\n311\t- name: COC Signal Validation\n312\t  plan_id: plan_005\n313\t  description: 'Three-part validation of Chunk Overlap Consistency as self-referential\n314\t    ACI update signal: (a) Spearman ρ between episode-level COC coverage fraction\n315\t    and binary task success, (b) ACI alpha_t convergence trajectory over evaluation\n316\t    episodes, (c) Ellipsoid volume comparison between ACI-adaptive and static calibration\n317\t    at matched marginal coverage.\n318\t\n319\t    '\n320\t  expected_results:\n321\t    coc_success_spearman_rho: 0.38\n322\t    coc_success_spearman_p_value: 0.003\n323\t    aci_convergence_episodes_to_0p02: 148\n324\t    aci_target_coverage: 0.9\n325\t    aci_achieved_coverage_at_convergence: 0.89\n326\t    aci_overshoot_peak_alpha: 0.22\n327\t    aci_steady_state_alpha: 0.11\n328\t    ellipsoid_volume_ratio_adaptive_vs_static: 0.847\n329\t    ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: 15.3\n330\t    static_calibration_coverage_achieved: 0.9\n331\t    adaptive_calibration_coverage_achieved: 0.89\n332\t  result_rationale: 'COC provides a per-step proxy for whether the model''s predictions\n333\t    are self-consistent across overlapping action chunks — high overlap agreement\n334\t    correlates with accurate predictions, low agreement with uncertain/inaccurate\n335\t    ones. However, the correlation with task success is attenuated (ρ=0.38, within\n336\t    [0.3, 0.5]) because: (1) task success is binary while COC is continuous, creating\n337\t    threshold effects; (2) a single bad step can fail a task regardless of high average\n338\t    COC; (3) some tasks succeed despite low COC through robustness of the underlying\n339\t    dynamics. The positive but weak correlation means COC is a \"promising\" self-referential\n340\t    signal but not \"reliable\" enough to be the primary ACI driver — episode-level\n341\t    success (when available) would be more informative. ACI converges but slowly (~148\n342\t    episodes, within expected range for weak signal) with initial overshoot (alpha\n343\t    peaks at 0.22 before settling to 0.11) because COC-based updates have high variance.\n344\t    The 15.3% ellipsoid narrowing (within [10, 20]) demonstrates that adaptive calibration\n345\t    produces tighter prediction sets than static — same coverage guarantee but more\n346\t    informative uncertainty regions. The narrowing is modest because K=10 covariance\n347\t    estimates limit how sharp the ellipsoids can get.\n348\t\n349\t    '\n350\t  tables:\n351\t  - name: 'Table 3: COC Signal Quality'\n352\t    content: '| Metric                              | Value  |\n353\t\n354\t      |-------------------------------------|--------|\n355\t\n356\t      | Spearman ρ (COC ↔ success)          | 0.38   |\n357\t\n358\t      | p-value                              | 0.003  |\n359\t\n360\t      | ACI convergence (episodes to ±0.02) | 148    |\n361\t\n362\t      | ACI achieved coverage               | 0.89   |\n363\t\n364\t      | Ellipsoid narrowing vs static        | 15.3%  |\n365\t\n366\t      '\n367\t  figures_description:\n368\t  - 'Fig 5a: Scatter plot of episode-level COC coverage fraction (x) vs task success\n369\t    (y, jittered binary). Spearman ρ=0.38 annotated. Clear positive trend but substantial\n370\t    scatter.'\n371\t  - 'Fig 5b: ACI convergence plot — alpha_t over 300 episodes with target coverage\n372\t    line (0.90). Shows initial overshoot, oscillation, and eventual convergence. Shaded\n373\t    band = 5 seed variance.'\n374\t  - 'Fig 5c: Ellipsoid volume CDF — adaptive (solid) vs static (dashed) at matched\n375\t    90% coverage. Adaptive curve shifted left, demonstrating 15.3% tighter volumes.'\n376\t  analysis: 'COC is validated as a positive but weak signal. ρ=0.38 is statistically\n377\t    significant (p=0.003) but far from the \"reliable\" threshold that would make COC\n378\t    a standalone monitoring tool. The paper should position COC as: \"a step toward\n379\t    fully self-referential uncertainty monitoring that shows promise but requires\n380\t    further development for deployment-grade reliability.\" ACI convergence demonstrates\n381\t    the theoretical guarantee holds in practice, but the slow convergence and overshoot\n382\t    suggest that COC''s noisy signal makes online adaptation sluggish. The ellipsoid\n383\t    narrowing result is the strongest evidence for ACI''s value — same coverage with\n384\t    tighter sets means more informative uncertainty features for CRM.\n385\t\n386\t    '\n387\t- name: A1 Ablation — CRM without ACI (Static Calibration)\n388\t  plan_id: plan_006\n389\t  description: 'Full CRM with per-DOF conformal features, but CGS thresholds fixed\n390\t    at calibration-set values — no online ACI adaptation. COC is not computed. Tests\n391\t    the marginal contribution of ACI.\n392\t\n393\t    '\n394\t  expected_results:\n395\t    libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 67.8\n396\t    libero_plus_gain_over_base: 5.5\n397\t    a1_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 2.7\n398\t    libero_nominal_success_rate: 93.4\n399\t    static_coverage_at_early_episodes: 0.93\n400\t    static_coverage_at_late_episodes: 0.82\n401\t  result_rationale: 'Without ACI, conformal thresholds are calibrated on the training\n402\t    distribution and remain fixed. Under LIBERO-Plus perturbations, the calibration\n403\t    set is no longer representative: early evaluation episodes see conservative over-coverage\n404\t    (0.93 vs target 0.90) because perturbations haven''t fully diverged from calibration\n405\t    conditions, but later episodes see under-coverage (0.82) as distribution shift\n406\t    accumulates. This miscalibration means CRM receives systematically biased uncertainty\n407\t    features — over-estimated for some perturbation types, under-estimated for others.\n408\t    The 2.7% gap to full ACR-VLA reflects that ACI''s online correction produces better-calibrated\n409\t    inputs for CRM, particularly for perturbation dimensions where the shift is gradual\n410\t    and ACI can track it (camera, lighting). For abrupt shifts (layout, language),\n411\t    ACI has less time to adapt, so the gap is smaller there. The result validates\n412\t    ACI''s contribution but shows it''s not the dominant factor — static conformal\n413\t    features still carry most of the correction signal.\n414\t\n415\t    '\n416\t  tables:\n417\t  - name: Ablation Table Row (A1)\n418\t    content: '| Variant                | LIBERO-Plus | Δ vs Full |\n419\t\n420\t      |-----------------------|-------------|-----------|\n421\t\n422\t      | A1: CRM, static CGS  | 67.8        | -2.7      |\n423\t\n424\t      | Full ACR-VLA          | 70.5        | —         |\n425\t\n426\t      '\n427\t  figures_description:\n428\t  - 'Part of Fig 3 (ablation chart). Additionally: supplementary figure showing coverage\n429\t    drift over episodes — static (drifting down) vs ACI-adaptive (hovering near 0.90).'\n430\t  analysis: 'ACI contributes ~2.7% absolute, which is meaningful but secondary to\n431\t    the conformal features themselves. The coverage drift analysis (0.93→0.82 for\n432\t    static) provides a clean mechanistic explanation for why adaptive calibration\n433\t    helps. This supports Claim 2 but with caveats — ACI is beneficial but not transformative.\n434\t\n435\t    '\n436\t- name: A2 Ablation — ACI without CRM (Sample Selection Only)\n437\t  plan_id: plan_007\n438\t  description: 'CGS + COC + ACI pipeline but instead of CRM, uses multivariate-CP-guided\n439\t    sample selection (extend ReconVLA''s approach to leverage per-DOF conformal radii\n440\t    for selecting best sample from K=10). No learned correction, just informed selection.\n441\t\n442\t    '\n443\t  expected_results:\n444\t    libero_plus_aggregate_success_rate: 66.9\n445\t    libero_plus_gain_over_base: 4.6\n446\t    a2_gap_to_full_acr_vla: 3.6\n447\t    libero_nominal_success_rate: 94.2\n448\t  result_rationale: 'Sample selection with multivariate CP features is a strict improvement\n449\t    over ReconVLA''s scalar selection — per-DOF conformal radii enable selecting the\n450\t    sample that is best specifically in the most-uncertain dimensions, rather than\n451\t    best in aggregate Euclidean distance. The 4.6% gain over base is substantial and\n452\t    validates that CGS features contain useful information. However, selection is\n453\t    fundamentally limited to choosing among the K=10 samples — it cannot synthesize\n454\t    an action better than any individual sample, which is CRM''s advantage. The 3.6%\n455\t    gap to full ACR-VLA quantifies CRM''s contribution as a learned synthesizer. Notably,\n456\t    the 0.6% nominal drop is much smaller than full ACR-VLA''s 2.2% — because sample\n457\t    selection is conservative (it only picks from generated samples, never modifying\n458\t    them), it''s less likely to degrade in-distribution performance. This creates\n459\t    an interesting trade-off for the paper to discuss.\n460\t\n461\t    '\n462\t  tables:\n463\t  - name: Ablation Table Row (A2)\n464\t    content: '| Variant               | LIBERO-Plus | LIBERO (nom.) | Δ Plus vs Full\n465\t      |\n466\t\n467\t      |----------------------|-------------|---------------|----------------|\n468\t\n469\t      | A2: Selection only   | 66.9        | 94.2          | -3.6           |\n470\t\n471\t      | Full ACR-VLA         | 70.5        | 92.6          | —              |\n472\t\n473\t      '\n474\t  figures_description:\n475\t  - 'Part of Fig 3. A2 bar annotated with note: ''selection-only — lower OOD gain\n476\t    but minimal nominal drop.'''\n477\t  analysis: 'A2 provides a useful reference point — it validates that CGS features\n478\t    enable better sample selection (extending ReconVLA), and quantifies CRM''s value\n479\t    as a learned corrector (+3.6%). The nominal preservation advantage of A2 is worth\n480\t    discussing as a practical deployment consideration: if nominal drop must be minimized,\n481\t    selection-only is safer.\n482\t\n483\t    '\n484\t- name: Per-Perturbation-Dimension Breakdown\n485\t  plan_id: plan_008\n486\t  description: 'Detailed ACR-VLA gain analysis across 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation dimensions,\n487\t    evaluated per-task (10 tasks × 7 dimensions = 70 cells). Statistical significance\n488\t    via paired bootstrap (1000 resamples, α=0.05) over 5 seeds.\n489\t\n490\t    '\n491\t  expected_results:\n492\t    camera_shift_gain: 12.6\n493\t    camera_shift_p_value: 0.001\n494\t    sensor_noise_gain: 10.8\n495\t    sensor_noise_p_value: 0.002\n496\t    robot_state_gain: 8.5\n497\t    robot_state_p_value: 0.008\n498\t    lighting_gain: 5.8\n499\t    lighting_p_value: 0.031\n500\t    background_gain: 3.2\n501\t    background_p_value: 0.112\n502\t    layout_gain: 0.9\n503\t    layout_p_value: 0.483\n504\t    language_gain: 0.9\n505\t    language_p_value: 0.501\n506\t    dims_with_significant_gain: 4\n507\t    dims_significant_list: camera_shift, sensor_noise, robot_state, lighting\n508\t  result_rationale: 'The per-perturbation structure directly reflects synthetic augmentation\n509\t    coverage. Camera jitter, Gaussian sensor noise, and robot-state perturbation are\n510\t    all covered by synthetic augmentation — CGS sees similar distribution shifts during\n511\t    calibration, producing well-calibrated conformal features that CRM can exploit.\n512\t    Camera shift shows the largest gain (+12.6%) because it primarily affects translational\n513\t    DOFs (x, y) where per-DOF conformal features are most discriminative — camera\n514\t    shift makes position uncertain while orientation remains relatively stable, and\n515\t    per-DOF features capture this asymmetry. Lighting (+5.8%) reaches significance\n516\t    because brightness changes create predictable patterns in flow-matching sample\n517\t    variance. Background changes (+3.2%) cause modest, diffuse uncertainty increase\n518\t    that CRM partially addresses, but the effect is not statistically significant\n519\t    at α=0.05. Layout (+0.9%) and language (+0.9%) are essentially unaddressed because\n520\t    these are semantic shifts that synthetic augmentation cannot cover — the calibration\n521\t    set contains no examples of rearranged furniture or paraphrased instructions,\n522\t    so CGS features under these perturbations are poorly calibrated and CRM cannot\n523\t    extract useful correction signal.\n524\t\n525\t    '\n526\t  tables:\n527\t  - name: 'Table 4: Per-Perturbation ACR-VLA Gains'\n528\t    content: '| Perturbation   | Base  | ACR-VLA | Δ     | p-value | Sig.? |\n529\t\n530\t      |---------------|-------|---------|-------|---------|-------|\n531\t\n532\t      | camera_shift  | 58.2  | 70.8    | +12.6 | 0.001   | ✓     |\n533\t\n534\t      | sensor_noise  | 55.6  | 66.4    | +10.8 | 0.002   | ✓     |\n535\t\n536\t      | robot_state   | 56.8  | 65.3    | +8.5  | 0.008   | ✓     |\n537\t\n538\t      | lighting      | 65.4  | 71.2    | +5.8  | 0.031   | ✓     |\n539\t\n540\t      | background    | 68.3  | 71.5    | +3.2  | 0.112   | ✗     |\n541\t\n542\t      | layout        | 72.1  | 73.0    | +0.9  | 0.483   | ✗     |\n543\t\n544\t      | language      | 71.5  | 72.4    | +0.9  | 0.501   | ✗     |\n545\t\n546\t      '\n547\t  - name: 'Table 4b: Per-Task × Per-Perturbation Heatmap Data (top-5 tasks by gain)'\n548\t    content: '| Task              | camera | noise | robot | light | bg   | layout\n549\t      | lang |\n550\t\n551\t      |-------------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|------|--------|------|\n552\t\n553\t      | pick_place_can    | +18.0  | +14.0 | +12.0 | +8.0  | +4.0 | +2.0   | +0.0\n554\t      |\n555\t\n556\t      | open_drawer       | +16.0  | +12.0 | +10.0 | +6.0  | +4.0 | +0.0   | +2.0\n557\t      |\n558\t\n559\t      | stack_blocks      | +14.0  | +14.0 | +10.0 | +8.0  | +2.0 | +0.0   | +0.0\n560\t      |\n561\t\n562\t      | close_microwave   | +12.0  | +10.0 | +8.0  | +6.0  | +4.0 | +2.0   | +0.0\n563\t      |\n564\t\n565\t      | turn_faucet       | +10.0  | +8.0  | +6.0  | +4.0  | +2.0 | +0.0   | +2.0\n566\t      |\n567\t\n568\t      '\n569\t  figures_description:\n570\t  - 'Fig 6: Radar chart showing ACR-VLA gain (%) across 7 perturbation dimensions.\n571\t    Clear asymmetry — gain concentrated in camera/noise/robot-state lobe, flat on\n572\t    layout/language.'\n573\t  - 'Fig 6b: 10×7 heatmap of per-task per-perturbation success rate deltas. Reveals\n574\t    task-perturbation interaction patterns.'\n575\t  - 'Fig 6c: Correlation between synthetic augmentation coverage (measured as calibration\n576\t    set overlap with each perturbation) and ACR-VLA gain. Strong positive correlation\n577\t    (r=0.91).'\n578\t  analysis: '4/7 dimensions with significant gain is within [3, 5]. This result is\n579\t    central to the median-tier narrative: ACR-VLA works well where calibration coverage\n580\t    exists, honestly fails where it doesn''t. The paper should present this transparency\n581\t    as a strength — \"we clearly identify the boundary of our method''s applicability\n582\t    and provide a mechanistic explanation.\" The correlation between augmentation coverage\n583\t    and gain (r=0.91) is a strong explanatory result that reviewers will appreciate.\n584\t    Layout/language as honest limitations, with ACI online adaptation as partial mitigation\n585\t    and future work on semantic augmentation as natural extension.\n586\t\n587\t    '\n588\t- name: A5 Ablation — CGS Joint Mahalanobis vs Per-DOF Bonferroni\n589\t  plan_id: plan_009\n590\t  description: 'Compare full CGS (joint Mahalanobis with Ledoit-Wolf shrunk covariance)\n591\t    vs per-DOF independent Bonferroni-corrected intervals. Tests whether multivariate\n592\t    CP''s joint structure provides value beyond simply having per-DOF information.\n593\t\n594\t    '\n595\t  expected_results:\n596\t    joint_mahalanobis_libero_plus: 70.5\n597\t    bonferroni_libero_plus: 69.1\n598\t    gap_joint_vs_bonferroni: 1.4\n599\t    bonferroni_coverage: 0.94\n600\t    joint_coverage: 0.9\n601\t    bonferroni_avg_ellipsoid_volume: 1.0\n602\t    joint_avg_ellipsoid_volume: 0.72\n603\t  result_rationale: 'Bonferroni correction treats each DOF independently and inflates\n604\t    thresholds to maintain joint coverage — producing conservative, hypercubic confidence\n605\t    regions that ignore cross-DOF covariance. Joint Mahalanobis with shrunk covariance\n606\t    produces ellipsoidal regions aligned with the actual covariance structure, yielding\n607\t    tighter (28% smaller volume) regions at the same coverage level. The 1.4% performance\n608\t    gap comes from CRM receiving more informative features: joint CGS features encode\n609\t    which DOF pairs co-vary (e.g., x and y under camera shift), enabling CRM to make\n610\t    correlated corrections across related DOFs. With Bonferroni, CRM sees each DOF''s\n611\t    uncertainty independently and must re-learn any cross-DOF correction patterns\n612\t    from the observation embedding alone. The gap is modest because K=10 Ledoit-Wolf\n613\t    covariance is noisy — the theoretical advantage of joint Mahalanobis is attenuated\n614\t    by covariance estimation error, and Bonferroni''s conservative margins partially\n615\t    compensate by providing wider \"safety padding.\"\n616\t\n617\t    '\n618\t  tables:\n619\t  - name: A5 Ablation Results\n620\t    content: '| CGS Variant         | LIBERO-Plus | Coverage | Avg. Volume |\n621\t\n622\t      |--------------------|-------------|----------|-------------|\n623\t\n624\t      | Joint Mahalanobis  | 70.5        | 0.90     | 0.72 (norm) |\n625\t\n626\t      | Per-DOF Bonferroni | 69.1        | 0.94     | 1.00 (norm) |\n627\t\n628\t      '\n629\t  figures_description:\n630\t  - 'Supplementary Fig S1: 2D slices of confidence regions — ellipsoidal (joint) vs\n631\t    rectangular (Bonferroni) for example action dimensions. Shows tighter ellipsoidal\n632\t    regions capturing covariance tilt.'\n633\t  analysis: 'The 1.4% gap is small but validates that joint multivariate CP provides\n634\t    value through covariance-aware regions. Worth reporting as a supplementary ablation\n635\t    to support the multivariate CP design choice. The over-coverage of Bonferroni\n636\t    (0.94 vs target 0.90) is an inherent inefficiency that the joint approach avoids.\n637\t\n638\t    '\n639\t- name: A6 Ablation — COC Signal vs Episode-Level Success for ACI\n640\t  plan_id: plan_010\n641\t  description: 'Compare ACI driven by COC (self-referential, per-step) vs ACI driven\n642\t    by episode-level binary task success (stronger signal, but requires ground-truth\n643\t    outcome and only updates once per episode).\n644\t\n645\t    '\n646\t  expected_results:\n647\t    coc_based_aci_libero_plus: 70.5\n648\t    episode_based_aci_libero_plus: 71.8\n649\t    gap_episode_vs_coc: 1.3\n650\t    coc_aci_convergence_episodes: 148\n651\t    episode_aci_convergence_episodes: 42\n652\t    coc_aci_coverage_variance: 0.018\n653\t    episode_aci_coverage_variance: 0.006\n654\t  result_rationale: 'Episode-level success is a stronger, lower-variance signal for\n655\t    ACI — it directly reflects whether the uncertainty calibration led to good outcomes.\n656\t    COC is noisy because chunk overlap consistency is only a proxy for prediction\n657\t    quality, and individual step-level COC values have high variance. The 1.3% gap\n658\t    reflects that episode-based ACI converges ~3.5x faster (42 vs 148 episodes) and\n659\t    maintains tighter coverage tracking (variance 0.006 vs 0.018). However, episode-level\n660\t    success requires ground-truth task outcomes — available in simulation but not\n661\t    in real deployment. This is the key argument for COC: it''s self-referential (no\n662\t    external supervision). The paper should present this as a trade-off: \"episode-level\n663\t    ACI is preferable when outcomes are available; COC-based ACI sacrifices ~1.3%\n664\t    performance for full self-referentiality.\" In the median tier, COC''s disadvantage\n665\t    is noticeable but not disqualifying — it still converges and provides meaningful\n666\t    adaptation, just more slowly and with more variance.\n667\t\n668\t    '\n669\t  tables:\n670\t  - name: 'A6 Ablation: ACI Signal Comparison'\n671\t    content: '| ACI Signal     | LIBERO-Plus | Convergence (eps) | Coverage Var. |\n672\t\n673\t      |---------------|-------------|-------------------|---------------|\n674\t\n675\t      | COC (self-ref)| 70.5        | 148               | 0.018         |\n676\t\n677\t      | Episode success| 71.8       | 42                | 0.006         |\n678\t\n679\t      '\n680\t  figures_description:\n681\t  - 'Supplementary Fig S2: Side-by-side ACI convergence plots — COC-driven (noisy,\n682\t    slow) vs episode-driven (smooth, fast). Both converge to target coverage but with\n683\t    very different dynamics.'\n684\t  analysis: 'Episode-based ACI is clearly superior when ground-truth is available.\n685\t    COC''s value proposition is self-referentiality for deployment scenarios without\n686\t    outcome feedback. The 1.3% gap and 3.5x convergence slowdown are honest costs\n687\t    of self-referentiality. Paper recommendation: use episode-level ACI as the default\n688\t    in simulation experiments; present COC as a deployment-oriented variant with known\n689\t    performance trade-offs. This supports repositioning COC as \"promising future work\"\n690\t    rather than the primary ACI signal.\n691\t\n692\t    '\n693\t- name: A7 Ablation — DiU Aleatoric vs CGS Epistemic Features\n694\t  plan_id: plan_011\n695\t  description: 'Replace CGS epistemic uncertainty features (45D) with DiU-style aleatoric\n696\t    uncertainty features (7D per-DOF variance from single forward pass). Also test\n697\t    combined epistemic+aleatoric (52D). Validates that epistemic (distribution-shift-sensitive)\n698\t    features are more useful than aleatoric (in-distribution-also-high) features for\n699\t    OOD correction.\n700\t\n701\t    '\n702\t  expected_results:\n703\t    cgs_epistemic_libero_plus: 70.5\n704\t    diu_aleatoric_libero_plus: 67.4\n705\t    combined_52d_libero_plus: 71.1\n706\t    gap_epistemic_vs_aleatoric: 3.1\n707\t    cgs_epistemic_libero_nominal: 92.6\n708\t    diu_aleatoric_libero_nominal: 93.9\n709\t    combined_52d_libero_nominal: 92.4\n710\t    diu_aleatoric_in_dist_variance: 0.048\n711\t    cgs_epistemic_in_dist_variance: 0.012\n712\t    diu_aleatoric_ood_variance: 0.063\n713\t    cgs_epistemic_ood_variance: 0.089\n714\t  result_rationale: 'DiU captures aleatoric uncertainty — inherent action ambiguity\n715\t    that exists even in-distribution. Under distribution shift, DiU''s signal increases\n716\t    only modestly (+31%, 0.048→0.063) because most of what DiU measures is irreducible\n717\t    noise. CGS epistemic features, in contrast, are low in-distribution (0.012) and\n718\t    spike dramatically under shift (+642%, 0.012→0.089), providing a clear signal\n719\t    for \"this is out-of-distribution.\" CRM conditioned on epistemic features can discriminate\n720\t    OOD steps that need correction from in-distribution steps that don''t. With aleatoric\n721\t    features, CRM sees consistently elevated uncertainty everywhere, reducing its\n722\t    ability to be selective. The 3.1% gap validates the epistemic-vs-aleatoric distinction.\n723\t    The combined 52D features provide a marginal +0.6% over epistemic alone — aleatoric\n724\t    features add some complementary information about inherent action ambiguity (useful\n725\t    for multi-modal tasks like placing objects with rotational symmetry), but the\n726\t    gain is small because most of the relevant signal is already captured by epistemic\n727\t    features. The lower nominal drop of DiU (0.9% vs 2.2% for CGS) reflects that aleatoric\n728\t    features are less \"aggressive\" — they don''t provoke large corrections because\n729\t    their signal doesn''t differentiate ID from OOD.\n730\t\n731\t    '\n732\t  tables:\n733\t  - name: 'A7 Ablation: Feature Type Comparison'\n734\t    content: '| Feature Type        | Dim | LIBERO (nom.) | LIBERO-Plus | Δ Plus vs\n735\t      Base |\n736\t\n737\t      |--------------------|-----|---------------|-------------|----------------|\n738\t\n739\t      | CGS epistemic       | 45  | 92.6          | 70.5        | +8.2           |\n740\t\n741\t      | DiU aleatoric       | 7   | 93.9          | 67.4        | +5.1           |\n742\t\n743\t      | Combined            | 52  | 92.4          | 71.1        | +8.8           |\n744\t\n745\t      '\n746\t  - name: 'A7: Signal Discrimination'\n747\t    content: '| Feature Source | In-Dist Var. | OOD Var. | Ratio (OOD/ID) |\n748\t\n749\t      |--------------|-------------|---------|----------------|\n750\t\n751\t      | CGS epistemic | 0.012       | 0.089   | 7.42           |\n752\t\n753\t      | DiU aleatoric | 0.048       | 0.063   | 1.31           |\n754\t\n755\t      '\n756\t  figures_description:\n757\t  - 'Supplementary Fig S3a: Violin plots of feature magnitude distributions (ID vs\n758\t    OOD) for epistemic and aleatoric features. CGS shows clear bimodal separation;\n759\t    DiU shows overlapping distributions.'\n760\t  - 'Supplementary Fig S3b: CRM correction magnitude conditioned on feature type —\n761\t    epistemic-conditioned CRM applies larger corrections OOD and smaller ID; aleatoric-conditioned\n762\t    CRM applies similar corrections everywhere.'\n763\t  analysis: 'The 7.42x OOD/ID ratio for epistemic features vs 1.31x for aleatoric\n764\t    validates the core design choice. This is one of the cleaner results — it mechanistically\n765\t    explains why flow-matching sample diversity (epistemic) is more informative than\n766\t    single-pass variance (aleatoric) for distribution shift detection. The combined\n767\t    52D result (+0.6% over epistemic alone) suggests that adding aleatoric features\n768\t    is marginally beneficial — worth mentioning but not worth the complexity for most\n769\t    applications.\n770\t\n771\t    '\n772\t- name: Feature Importance Analysis\n773\t  plan_id: plan_012\n774\t  description: 'Gradient-based feature importance: compute |∂correction/∂feature|\n775\t    averaged over evaluation episodes, broken down by perturbation type. Identifies\n776\t    which of the 45 CGS features drive CRM''s corrections for each perturbation dimension.\n777\t\n778\t    '\n779\t  expected_results:\n780\t    top_features_camera_shift: per-DOF std (x, y) — 34% of total gradient; conformal\n781\t      radius (x, y) — 22%\n782\t    top_features_sensor_noise: per-DOF std (all DOFs) — 41%; ellipsoid volume — 18%\n783\t    top_features_robot_state: conformal radius (joint 1-4) — 38%; per-DOF std (joint\n784\t      1-4) — 25%\n785\t    top_features_lighting: per-DOF std (x, y, z) — 29%; ellipsoid volume — 21%\n786\t    per_dof_std_importance_share: 0.42\n787\t    conformal_radius_importance_share: 0.31\n788\t    ellipsoid_volume_importance_share: 0.14\n789\t    cross_dof_covariance_importance_share: 0.13\n790\t  result_rationale: 'Per-DOF std features are the most important (42% of gradient\n791\t    magnitude) because they directly encode \"how uncertain is this specific DOF\" —\n792\t    the most actionable information for dimension-selective correction. Conformal\n793\t    radius features rank second (31%) because they encode calibrated confidence intervals\n794\t    that are more robust than raw std to distributional assumptions. Ellipsoid volume\n795\t    (14%) is a global uncertainty summary — useful for CRM''s gating mechanism (how\n796\t    much total correction to apply) but less informative for dimension selection.\n797\t    Cross-DOF covariance features (13%) capture correlation structure; their moderate\n798\t    importance reflects that K=10 covariance estimates are noisy, limiting the information\n799\t    content of off-diagonal terms. The perturbation-specific patterns are illuminating:\n800\t    camera shift activates translational DOF features (x, y) because it primarily\n801\t    affects workspace position estimation; robot-state perturbation activates joint-specific\n802\t    conformal radii because it directly perturbs joint configurations; sensor noise\n803\t    activates broad std features because it diffusely increases all DOF uncertainties.\n804\t\n805\t    '\n806\t  tables:\n807\t  - name: 'Table 5: Feature Importance by Category'\n808\t    content: '| Feature Category    | Importance Share | Primary Perturbations |\n809\t\n810\t      |--------------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n811\t\n812\t      | Per-DOF std (7D)   | 42%             | noise, camera, light |\n813\t\n814\t      | Conformal radius (7D)| 31%           | robot-state, camera  |\n815\t\n816\t      | Ellipsoid volume (1D)| 14%           | all (gating)         |\n817\t\n818\t      | Cross-DOF cov (30D) | 13%            | robot-state          |\n819\t\n820\t      '\n821\t  figures_description:\n822\t  - 'Fig 7a: Stacked bar chart — feature importance share by category, one bar per\n823\t    perturbation dimension. Shows distinct feature activation patterns per perturbation\n824\t    type.'\n825\t  - 'Fig 7b: 45-feature × 7-perturbation heatmap of gradient magnitudes. Reveals sparse,\n826\t    interpretable activation patterns — CRM learns perturbation-specific feature usage\n827\t    without explicit supervision.'\n828\t  analysis: 'The feature importance analysis validates the dimension-selective correction\n829\t    mechanism: CRM learns to attend to different uncertainty features for different\n830\t    perturbation types, without explicit supervision about which perturbation is active.\n831\t    This is a compelling interpretability result for the paper. The 42%+31% = 73%\n832\t    importance of per-DOF features (std + conformal radius) supports Claim 1''s emphasis\n833\t    on per-DOF structure, even though the A4 ablation showed only +2.1% over scalar\n834\t    — the mechanism is clearly there, even if the aggregate performance gain is modest.\n835\t\n836\t    '\n837\t- name: SnapFlow Integration (K=10 × 1-step)\n838\t  plan_id: plan_013\n839\t  description: 'Replace K=10 full denoising trajectories with K=10 single-step distilled\n840\t    generation via SnapFlow-style consistency distillation. Test: (a) inference speedup,\n841\t    (b) sample diversity preservation, (c) downstream ACR-VLA performance.\n842\t\n843\t    '\n844\t  expected_results:\n845\t    full_denoising_inference_ms: 118\n846\t    snapflow_1step_inference_ms: 38\n847\t    speedup_factor: 3.1\n848\t    diversity_trace_ratio: 0.83\n849\t    full_denoising_libero_plus: 70.5\n850\t    snapflow_libero_plus: 69.8\n851\t    performance_drop: 0.7\n852\t    snapflow_covariance_frobenius_error: 0.21\n853\t  result_rationale: 'Single-step distilled generation produces samples ~3.1x faster\n854\t    but with reduced diversity: the trace ratio of 0.83 means 1-step samples capture\n855\t    83% of the covariance structure of full denoising samples. The 17% diversity loss\n856\t    comes from the distillation process collapsing minor modes in the action distribution\n857\t    — the dominant mode is well-preserved but tail samples are pulled inward. This\n858\t    propagates to a modest 0.7% ACR-VLA performance drop because the CGS features\n859\t    computed from less-diverse samples have slightly attenuated covariance structure,\n860\t    reducing the per-DOF signal quality. The Frobenius error of 0.21 (normalized)\n861\t    in covariance estimation means the Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage target absorbs more weight,\n862\t    further compressing the estimated covariance toward the identity-like shrinkage\n863\t    target. The practical trade-off is favorable: 3.1x speedup for 0.7% accuracy cost,\n864\t    making real-time deployment more feasible. This is a supplementary result that\n865\t    strengthens the practical narrative without affecting claims.\n866\t\n867\t    '\n868\t  tables:\n869\t  - name: 'Table S1: SnapFlow Integration'\n870\t    content: '| Sampling Method     | K=10 Time (ms) | Diversity | LIBERO-Plus | Δ   |\n871\t\n872\t      |--------------------|----------------|-----------|-------------|-----|\n873\t\n874\t      | Full denoising     | 118            | 1.00      | 70.5        | —   |\n875\t\n876\t      | SnapFlow 1-step    | 38             | 0.83      | 69.8        | -0.7|\n877\t\n878\t      '\n879\t  figures_description:\n880\t  - 'Supplementary Fig S4: Pareto plot — inference time vs LIBERO-Plus success rate.\n881\t    SnapFlow point near the Pareto frontier, showing favorable speed-accuracy trade-off.'\n882\t  analysis: SnapFlow integration is a practical engineering contribution. The 0.7%\n883\t    drop is small enough that the 3.1x speedup is clearly worthwhile for deployment.\n884\t    The diversity reduction is the primary concern — if future work increases K beyond\n885\t    10, SnapFlow's diversity loss may compound. Report as supplementary result demonstrating\n886\t    deployment feasibility.\n887\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018i52FenMRvkSHT69i2U2VC","session_id":"cb8be02c-35b7-47dd-bfab-cb2c629ce5a2","uuid":"6372a3d4-d138-4402-85f5-ab991ab66274","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:15:03.273Z"}
