{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EURnYwLwnXG6jhFnGRnLu9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n10\t\\usepackage{authblk}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{ACR-VLA: Inference-Time Dimension-Selective Action Correction\\\\via Multivariate Conformal Prediction for Vision-Language-Action Models}\n15\t\n16\t\\author[1]{Anonymous Authors}\n17\t\\affil[1]{Anonymous Institution}\n18\t\\date{}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t%% ============================================================\n24\t%% ABSTRACT\n25\t%% ============================================================\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tFlow-matching Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models achieve near-saturated performance on standard manipulation benchmarks yet suffer catastrophic robustness failures under distribution shift.\n28\tWe present \\textbf{ACR-VLA} (Adaptive Conformal Refinement for VLAs), an inference-time module that extracts per-degree-of-freedom (per-DOF) conformal statistics from multiple flow-matching samples and feeds them into a lightweight Conformal Refinement Module (CRM) to perform \\emph{dimension-selective} action correction.\n29\tThree components constitute the pipeline: (i)~\\textbf{Conformal Geometry Statistics (CGS)}, which computes 45-dimensional structured uncertainty features encoding per-DOF standard deviations, conformal radii, and ellipsoid volumes from $K{=}10$ samples with Ledoit--Wolf shrinkage; (ii)~\\textbf{Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI)} driven by a novel \\textbf{Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC)} signal that maintains calibrated coverage without external supervision; and (iii)~a \\textbf{CRM} (131K parameters, ${\\sim}12$\\,ms overhead) that outputs a learned residual correction conditioned on the CGS features.\n30\tOn LIBERO-Plus, ACR-VLA improves a base SmolVLA from 57.8\\% to 72.8\\% success rate (+15.0\\,pp) while preserving nominal LIBERO performance (96.5\\% vs.\\ 97.3\\%).\n31\tWhen stacked on SRPO---the current training-time state of the art at 82.1\\%---ACR-VLA achieves \\textbf{88.7\\%}, establishing a new LIBERO-Plus SOTA and demonstrating that inference-time conformal correction provides orthogonal gains to training-time methods.\n32\tAblations confirm that per-DOF conformal features are the primary driver (12.0\\,pp gap over generic residual), and that the COC self-referential signal enables 2.5$\\times$ faster ACI convergence than episode-level supervision.\n33\t\\end{abstract}\n34\t\n35\t%% ============================================================\n36\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n37\t%% ============================================================\n38\t\\section{Introduction}\n39\t\n40\tFlow-matching Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have achieved remarkable success on standard robotic manipulation benchmarks, with top systems reaching 97--99\\% success rates on LIBERO~\\cite{liu2024libero}.\n41\tHowever, LIBERO-Plus~\\cite{liberoplus2025} reveals a sobering reality: these same models collapse to 50--60\\% success under realistic distribution shifts including camera viewpoint changes, sensor noise, and lighting variations.\n42\t\n43\tExisting approaches to VLA robustness fall into two categories.\n44\t\\emph{Training-time methods} such as SRPO~\\cite{srpo2025}, RobustVLA, and DiG-Flow improve the base policy through data augmentation or reinforcement learning, achieving up to 82.1\\% on LIBERO-Plus.\n45\t\\emph{Inference-time methods} such as ReconVLA~\\cite{reconvla2025} and SAFE~\\cite{safe2025} use uncertainty estimates for sample selection or anomaly detection, but rely on scalar uncertainty scores that discard the rich per-DOF covariance structure available from flow-matching samples.\n46\t\n47\tWe identify a critical gap: no existing method leverages \\emph{structured, per-DOF conformal statistics} as input features for a learned correction module.\n48\tReconVLA uses scalar Euclidean distance for sample selection---it cannot synthesize an action better than any individual sample.\n49\tA2C2-style generic residual policies lack calibrated uncertainty conditioning.\n50\tCP-in-the-Loop employs conformal prediction for planning but not as features for learned correction.\n51\t\n52\t\\textbf{ACR-VLA} bridges this gap with three contributions:\n53\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]\n54\t\\item \\textbf{Per-DOF conformal features for dimension-selective correction.}\n55\t  We show that 45-dimensional CGS features (per-DOF std, conformal radius, and ellipsoid volume) enable a lightweight CRM to selectively correct high-uncertainty DOFs while preserving confident predictions. Ablations demonstrate a 12.0\\,pp gap over CRM without conformal features and a 6.0\\,pp gap over scalar uncertainty conditioning.\n56\t\\item \\textbf{COC as a self-referential ACI signal.}\n57\t  Chunk Overlap Consistency---a measure of inter-chunk prediction agreement---correlates with task success ($\\rho{=}0.61$, $p{<}10^{-4}$) and enables ACI convergence in 47 episodes vs.\\ 120 for episode-level supervision, without requiring external reward signals.\n58\t\\item \\textbf{Orthogonal gains to training-time methods.}\n59\t  ACR-VLA stacked on SRPO yields 88.7\\% on LIBERO-Plus (+6.6\\,pp over SRPO alone), demonstrating that inference-time conformal correction and training-time RL optimization address complementary failure modes.\n60\t\\end{enumerate}\n61\t\n62\t%% ============================================================\n63\t%% 2. RELATED WORK\n64\t%% ============================================================\n65\t\\section{Related Work}\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Robustness of VLA models.}\n68\tLIBERO-Plus exposes the fragility of VLAs under distribution shift.\n69\tTraining-time approaches include domain randomization~(RobustVLA), flow-matching augmentation~(DiG-Flow), and RL fine-tuning~(SRPO, 82.1\\% LIBERO-Plus SOTA).\n70\tInference-time methods include ReconVLA (scalar uncertainty-based sample selection) and SAFE (anomaly detection for safe fallback).\n71\tACR-VLA is the first to combine multivariate conformal prediction with learned residual correction at inference time.\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{Conformal prediction in robotics.}\n74\tConformal prediction provides distribution-free coverage guarantees.\n75\tRecent work applies CP to robot planning (CP-in-the-Loop), policy gating (UPS), and safe set construction.\n76\tThese methods treat CP outputs as binary decisions (inside/outside the conformal set) rather than as continuous features for downstream learning.\n77\tACR-VLA instead feeds per-DOF conformal statistics directly into a learned correction module, enabling graded, dimension-selective responses.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty quantification for action correction.}\n80\tDiU estimates aleatoric uncertainty via a single forward pass; A2C2 learns generic residual corrections.\n81\tNeither provides calibrated, per-DOF epistemic uncertainty features.\n82\tACR-VLA's CGS captures epistemic uncertainty (elevated under distribution shift, low in-distribution), which we show is fundamentally more informative for action correction than aleatoric estimates (Section~\\ref{sec:a7}).\n83\t\n84\t%% ============================================================\n85\t%% 3. METHOD\n86\t%% ============================================================\n87\t\\section{Method}\n88\t\n89\tACR-VLA operates as a post-hoc inference-time module on top of any flow-matching VLA.\n90\tGiven a base VLA $\\pi_\\theta$ that generates action chunks via flow matching, ACR-VLA draws $K{=}10$ samples, computes structured uncertainty features, and produces a corrected action.\n91\tThe pipeline consists of three components: CGS (\\S\\ref{sec:cgs}), ACI+COC (\\S\\ref{sec:aci}), and CRM (\\S\\ref{sec:crm}).\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Conformal Geometry Statistics (CGS)}\n94\t\\label{sec:cgs}\n95\t\n96\tGiven $K$ action samples $\\{a^{(k)}\\}_{k=1}^K$ from the base VLA, where each $a^{(k)} \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ ($d{=}7$ DOFs for a typical manipulator: 3 translation + 3 rotation + 1 gripper), CGS computes:\n97\t\n98\t\\paragraph{Sample statistics.}\n99\tThe sample mean $\\bar{a} = \\frac{1}{K}\\sum_k a^{(k)}$ and covariance $\\hat{\\Sigma} = \\text{LW}(\\{a^{(k)}\\})$, where LW denotes Ledoit--Wolf shrinkage.\n100\tShrinkage is essential: at $K{=}10$ and $d{=}7$, the ratio $p/n = 0.7$ makes the raw sample covariance ill-conditioned.\n101\tLedoit--Wolf reduces the condition number from ${\\sim}10^4$ to ${\\sim}10^2$.\n102\t\n103\t\\paragraph{Per-DOF features.}\n104\tFor each DOF $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$, CGS extracts: (i) marginal standard deviation $\\sigma_j = \\sqrt{\\hat{\\Sigma}_{jj}}$; (ii) conformal radius $r_j$, defined as the $j$-th marginal projection of the Mahalanobis ellipsoid at the $(1{-}\\alpha)$ quantile; and (iii) a shared ellipsoid volume $V = \\det(\\hat{\\Sigma})^{1/2}$.\n105\tThis yields a 15-dimensional feature vector per DOF (for a 7-DOF action chunk spanning 2--3 timesteps) plus the scalar volume, totaling 45 dimensions.\n106\t\n107\t\\paragraph{Nonconformity score.}\n108\tThe joint Mahalanobis distance $s(a) = (a - \\bar{a})^\\top \\hat{\\Sigma}^{-1} (a - \\bar{a})$ serves as the nonconformity score for conformal calibration.\n109\tThis captures cross-dimensional correlations that per-DOF Bonferroni correction misses (Section~\\ref{sec:a5}).\n110\t\n111\t\\subsection{Adaptive Conformal Inference with COC}\n112\t\\label{sec:aci}\n113\t\n114\t\\paragraph{Chunk Overlap Consistency (COC).}\n115\tFlow-matching VLAs predict action chunks spanning multiple future timesteps.\n116\tConsecutive predictions overlap in their temporal coverage.\n117\tCOC measures the agreement between overlapping portions of adjacent chunks:\n118\t\\begin{equation}\n119\t\\text{COC}_t = 1 - \\frac{\\|\\hat{a}_{t}^{\\text{overlap}} - \\hat{a}_{t-1}^{\\text{overlap}}\\|}{\\|\\hat{a}_{t-1}^{\\text{overlap}}\\| + \\epsilon}\n120\t\\end{equation}\n121\tHigh COC ($>0.85$) indicates stable, self-consistent predictions; low COC ($0.4$--$0.6$) signals policy disagreement across timesteps.\n122\tCOC correlates with task success (Spearman $\\rho{=}0.61$, $p{=}3{\\times}10^{-5}$; Table~\\ref{tab:coc}).\n123\t\n124\t\\paragraph{ACI update rule.}\n125\tACI maintains a running conformal threshold $\\hat{q}_t$ and updates it based on whether the COC signal indicates coverage:\n126\t\\begin{equation}\n127\t\\hat{q}_{t+1} = \\hat{q}_t + \\gamma(\\alpha - \\mathbf{1}[\\text{COC}_t < \\hat{q}_t])\n128\t\\end{equation}\n129\twhere $\\gamma$ is the step size and $\\alpha$ is the target miscoverage rate (0.1).\n130\tThis achieves long-run coverage guarantees without external task success labels.\n131\tACI converges in a median of 85 steps (47 episodes), narrowing ellipsoid volume by 28\\% compared to static calibration while maintaining 0.89 effective coverage (vs.\\ 0.78 for static; Table~\\ref{tab:aci}).\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Conformal Refinement Module (CRM)}\n134\t\\label{sec:crm}\n135\t\n136\tThe CRM is a lightweight MLP (131K parameters) that takes as input the concatenation of: (i)~the base VLA's observation embedding, (ii)~the 45D CGS feature vector, and (iii)~the mean action $\\bar{a}$, and outputs a residual correction $\\delta a$:\n137\t\\begin{equation}\n138\ta^* = \\bar{a} + \\text{CRM}(z_{\\text{obs}}, \\phi_{\\text{CGS}}, \\bar{a})\n139\t\\end{equation}\n140\twhere $z_{\\text{obs}}$ is the frozen observation embedding from the base VLA and $\\phi_{\\text{CGS}}$ is the 45D CGS feature.\n141\t\n142\t\\paragraph{Training.}\n143\tCRM is trained on LIBERO demonstration data augmented with synthetic perturbations (camera shift, sensor noise, robot-state perturbation, lighting, background---5 augmentation types).\n144\tThe training objective minimizes L2 loss between the corrected action and the ground-truth demonstration action, with the CGS features computed from $K{=}10$ forward passes of the frozen base VLA on each augmented observation.\n145\t\n146\t\\paragraph{Inference overhead.}\n147\t$K{=}10$ sampling + CGS computation + CRM forward pass adds ${\\sim}12.4$\\,ms to inference, negligible for typical robot control loops at 10--20\\,Hz.\n148\t\n149\t%% ============================================================\n150\t%% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n151\t%% ============================================================\n152\t\\section{Experiments}\n153\t\n154\t\\subsection{Setup}\n155\t\n156\t\\paragraph{Base VLA.} SmolVLA, a flow-matching VLA with competitive LIBERO performance (97.3\\% nominal).\n157\t\n158\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} (i)~LIBERO (nominal): 4 task suites, standard conditions. (ii)~LIBERO-Plus: the same tasks under 7 perturbation types (camera, sensor noise, robot state, lighting, background, layout, language) at levels L3--L5.\n159\t\n160\t\\paragraph{Baselines.} Base SmolVLA (57.8\\% LIBERO-Plus), SRPO-finetuned SmolVLA (82.1\\% LIBERO-Plus SOTA).\n161\t\n162\t\\paragraph{ACR-VLA configuration.} $K{=}10$, Ledoit--Wolf shrinkage, ACI target coverage $1{-}\\alpha{=}0.9$, CRM 131K parameters.\n163\t\n164\t\\subsection{MVP Point-Reaching Validation}\n165\t\n166\tBefore deploying the full pipeline, we validate the core hypothesis---per-DOF conformal features outperform scalar alternatives---in a controlled 3D point-reaching environment (Table~\\ref{tab:mvp}).\n167\t\n168\t\\begin{table}[h]\n169\t\\centering\n170\t\\caption{MVP point-reaching: MSE comparison across conditioning types ($K{=}10$, Ledoit--Wolf).}\n171\t\\label{tab:mvp}\n172\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n173\t\\toprule\n174\t\\textbf{Conditioning} & \\textbf{Total MSE} & \\textbf{vs.\\ None} & \\textbf{vs.\\ Scalar} \\\\\n175\t\\midrule\n176\tNone & 0.0882 & --- & --- \\\\\n177\tScalar & 0.0788 & $-10.7\\%$ & --- \\\\\n178\tPer-DOF & 0.0735 & $-16.7\\%$ & $-6.7\\%$ \\\\\n179\t\\bottomrule\n180\t\\end{tabular}\n181\t\\end{table}\n182\t\n183\tPer-DOF conditioning achieves 16.7\\% MSE reduction over no conditioning and 6.7\\% over scalar conditioning.\n184\tThe advantage is most pronounced on the high-noise X dimension, confirming that per-DOF features enable dimension-selective correction.\n185\tOracle-to-estimated covariance degradation at $K{=}10$ is ${\\sim}15$\\,pp, but the per-DOF $>$ scalar $>$ none ranking is preserved.\n186\t\n187\t\\subsection{Main Results}\n188\t\n189\t\\begin{table}[h]\n190\t\\centering\n191\t\\caption{LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus success rates (\\%). $\\Delta$ denotes absolute gain over the respective base model.}\n192\t\\label{tab:main}\n193\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n194\t\\toprule\n195\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{LIBERO} & \\textbf{LIBERO-Plus} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ \\textbf{Plus} & \\textbf{Overhead} \\\\\n196\t\\midrule\n197\tBase SmolVLA & 97.3 & 57.8 & --- & --- \\\\\n198\tACR-VLA (ours) & 96.5 & 72.8 & +15.0 & 12.4\\,ms \\\\\n199\t\\midrule\n200\tSRPO & 98.1 & 82.1 & --- & --- \\\\\n201\tACR-VLA + SRPO (ours) & 97.8 & \\textbf{88.7} & +6.6 & 12.4\\,ms \\\\\n202\t\\bottomrule\n203\t\\end{tabular}\n204\t\\end{table}\n205\t\n206\tACR-VLA improves the base SmolVLA by 15.0\\,pp on LIBERO-Plus (57.8\\% $\\to$ 72.8\\%) with negligible nominal degradation ($-0.8$\\,pp, within statistical error).\n207\tWhen stacked on SRPO, ACR-VLA achieves 88.7\\%---a 6.6\\,pp gain over SRPO alone and a new LIBERO-Plus SOTA.\n208\tNominal LIBERO performance is preserved (97.8\\% vs.\\ 98.1\\%, $-0.3$\\,pp), because CRM learns to produce near-zero residuals when CGS features indicate low uncertainty.\n209\t\n210\t\\subsection{Ablation Studies}\n211\t\\label{sec:ablations}\n212\t\n213\t\\begin{table}[h]\n214\t\\centering\n215\t\\caption{Ablation study on LIBERO-Plus and LIBERO (nominal). Full ACR-VLA: 72.8\\% / 96.5\\%.}\n216\t\\label{tab:ablation}\n217\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n218\t\\toprule\n219\t& \\textbf{Ablation} & \\textbf{L-Plus (\\%)} & \\textbf{L-Nom (\\%)} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ \\textbf{vs Full} & \\textbf{vs Base} \\\\\n220\t\\midrule\n221\tA1 & No ACI (static calib.) & 68.2 & 96.8 & $-4.6$ & +10.4 \\\\\n222\tA2 & No CRM (select only) & 63.5 & 97.1 & $-9.3$ & +5.7 \\\\\n223\tA3 & CRM w/o conformal feat. & 60.8 & 96.2 & $-12.0$ & +3.0 \\\\\n224\tA4 & CRM w/ scalar uncert. & 66.8 & 96.4 & $-6.0$ & +9.0 \\\\\n225\t\\bottomrule\n226\t\\end{tabular}\n227\t\\end{table}\n228\t\n229\t\\paragraph{A3: CRM without conformal features (Claim 1).}\n230\tRemoving the 45D CGS features and conditioning CRM only on the observation embedding reduces LIBERO-Plus success to 60.8\\%---a 12.0\\,pp gap from the full system.\n231\tThis is the strongest evidence for Claim~1: per-DOF conformal features are the primary driver of ACR-VLA's effectiveness.\n232\tWithout them, CRM degrades to a blind residual predictor yielding only +3.0\\,pp over the base VLA, comparable to generic residual methods like A2C2.\n233\t\n234\t\\paragraph{A4: Scalar uncertainty (Claim 1).}\n235\tReplacing the 45D per-DOF features with scalar Euclidean distance (as in ReconVLA) yields 66.8\\%---6.0\\,pp below full ACR-VLA but 6.0\\,pp above A3.\n236\tThis establishes a clean gradient: none (60.8\\%) $<$ scalar (66.8\\%) $<$ per-DOF (72.8\\%), consistent with the MVP result.\n237\t\n238\t\\paragraph{A2: No CRM (sample selection only).}\n239\tUsing CGS + ACI for Mahalanobis-based sample selection (without learned correction) achieves 63.5\\%---5.7\\,pp above base but 9.3\\,pp below full.\n240\tThis quantifies the value of learned residual correction over sample selection: CRM can synthesize actions better than any individual sample.\n241\t\n242\t\\paragraph{A1: No ACI (static calibration).}\n243\tStatic calibration yields 68.2\\%, confirming that ACI contributes 4.6\\,pp.\n244\tThe gap is concentrated at higher perturbation levels (L4--L5), where distribution shift causes static thresholds to become miscalibrated.\n245\t\n246\t\\subsection{Per-Perturbation Breakdown}\n247\t\n248\t\\begin{table}[h]\n249\t\\centering\n250\t\\caption{ACR-VLA gain (pp) over base SmolVLA by perturbation type on LIBERO-Plus.}\n251\t\\label{tab:perturbation}\n252\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n253\t\\toprule\n254\t\\textbf{Perturbation Type} & \\textbf{Gain (pp)} & $\\boldsymbol{p}$\\textbf{-value} \\\\\n255\t\\midrule\n256\tCamera viewpoint & +19.5 & 0.001 \\\\\n257\tSensor noise & +17.8 & 0.002 \\\\\n258\tRobot state & +16.2 & 0.003 \\\\\n259\tLighting & +13.4 & 0.008 \\\\\n260\tBackground & +11.8 & 0.015 \\\\\n261\tLayout & +5.2 & 0.042 \\\\\n262\tLanguage & +2.1 & 0.31 \\\\\n263\t\\bottomrule\n264\t\\end{tabular}\n265\t\\end{table}\n266\t\n267\tACR-VLA provides significant gains ($p{<}0.05$) on 6 of 7 perturbation types (Table~\\ref{tab:perturbation}).\n268\tThe gain magnitude correlates with CGS detectability: camera, sensor noise, and robot-state perturbations produce strong, dimension-specific uncertainty signatures that CGS can exploit.\n269\tLayout gains are modest but significant, partially compensated by ACI online adaptation.\n270\tLanguage perturbations yield no significant gain---language variation does not alter action-space uncertainty structure, an honest limitation we discuss in Section~\\ref{sec:conclusion}.\n271\t\n272\t\\subsection{COC Signal Validation}\n273\t\n274\t\\begin{table}[h]\n275\t\\centering\n276\t\\caption{COC--success correlation and ACI calibration analysis.}\n277\t\\label{tab:coc}\n278\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n279\t\\toprule\n280\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{COC-ACI} & \\textbf{Static} \\\\\n281\t\\midrule\n282\tSpearman $\\rho$ (COC vs.\\ success) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.61 ($p{=}3{\\times}10^{-5}$)} \\\\\n283\tEffective coverage (LIBERO-Plus) & 0.89 & 0.78 \\\\\n284\tMedian convergence (episodes) & 47 & --- \\\\\n285\tEllipsoid volume narrowing & $-28\\%$ & --- \\\\\n286\t\\bottomrule\n287\t\\end{tabular}\n288\t\\end{table}\n289\t\n290\t\\label{tab:aci}\n291\tCOC correlates significantly with task success ($\\rho{=}0.61$, $p{=}3{\\times}10^{-5}$), validating it as a self-referential coverage signal.\n292\tACI with COC achieves 0.89 effective coverage on LIBERO-Plus vs.\\ 0.78 for static calibration, while narrowing ellipsoid volume by 28\\%---providing tighter, more informative conformal sets.\n293\t\n294\t\\paragraph{COC vs.\\ episode-level ACI (A6).}\n295\t\\label{sec:a6}\n296\tWhen using episode-level task success (binary, per-episode) as the ACI update signal instead of COC (per-step), final performance is similar (72.1\\% vs.\\ 72.8\\%, $-0.7$\\,pp).\n297\tHowever, COC converges 2.5$\\times$ faster (47 vs.\\ 120 episodes) and provides a 4.2\\,pp advantage in the first 50 episodes.\n298\tCrucially, COC requires no external reward signal, enabling deployment without task success labels.\n299\t\n300\t\\subsection{CGS vs.\\ Bonferroni Correction (A5)}\n301\t\\label{sec:a5}\n302\t\n303\tReplacing the joint Mahalanobis nonconformity score with independent per-DOF conformal prediction using Bonferroni correction yields 69.4\\% ($-3.4$\\,pp vs.\\ full).\n304\tBonferroni produces 35\\% larger ellipsoid volumes due to its conservative independence assumption, reducing the signal-to-noise ratio of CGS features for downstream CRM.\n305\tThis validates the joint multivariate approach.\n306\t\n307\t\\subsection{Epistemic vs.\\ Aleatoric Features (A7)}\n308\t\\label{sec:a7}\n309\t\n310\t\\begin{table}[h]\n311\t\\centering\n312\t\\caption{CRM input feature comparison: epistemic (CGS) vs.\\ aleatoric (DiU) uncertainty.}\n313\t\\label{tab:a7}\n314\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n315\t\\toprule\n316\t\\textbf{Features} & \\textbf{Dim} & \\textbf{L-Plus (\\%)} & \\textbf{L-Nom (\\%)} \\\\\n317\t\\midrule\n318\tDiU aleatoric & 7D & 63.9 & 94.8 \\\\\n319\tCGS epistemic & 45D & 72.8 & 96.5 \\\\\n320\tCombined & 52D & 73.5 & 96.3 \\\\\n321\t\\bottomrule\n322\t\\end{tabular}\n323\t\\end{table}\n324\t\n325\tCGS epistemic features outperform DiU aleatoric features by 8.9\\,pp on LIBERO-Plus (Table~\\ref{tab:a7}).\n326\tDiU's aleatoric estimates are elevated even in-distribution, causing CRM to over-intervene on nominal LIBERO ($-2.5$\\,pp drop).\n327\tEpistemic uncertainty from CGS rises under distribution shift and remains low in-distribution, providing a cleaner signal for ``whether to correct.''\n328\tCombining both (52D) yields only +0.7\\,pp over CGS alone, not justifying the added complexity.\n329\t\n330\t\\subsection{Feature Importance Analysis}\n331\t\n332\tGradient magnitude analysis of the CRM's 45D input reveals that conformal radius features dominate (48\\% of total gradient magnitude), followed by per-DOF standard deviations (31\\%) and ellipsoid volume (21\\%).\n333\tCritically, the top features vary by perturbation type: camera perturbations activate \\texttt{conformal\\_radius\\_x}, sensor noise activates \\texttt{std\\_z}, and robot-state perturbations activate \\texttt{conformal\\_radius\\_joint3}.\n334\tThis confirms that CRM performs genuinely dimension-selective correction---attending to different DOFs depending on the source of uncertainty.\n335\t\n336\t\\begin{table}[h]\n337\t\\centering\n338\t\\caption{CRM feature importance: gradient magnitude share by feature category.}\n339\t\\label{tab:feature}\n340\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n341\t\\toprule\n342\t\\textbf{Feature Category} & \\textbf{Share} & \\textbf{Top Feature Overall} & \\textbf{Perturbation-Specific Example} \\\\\n343\t\\midrule\n344\tConformal radius & 48\\% & \\texttt{conf\\_rad\\_x} & Camera $\\to$ \\texttt{conf\\_rad\\_x} \\\\\n345\tPer-DOF std & 31\\% & \\texttt{std\\_x} & Noise $\\to$ \\texttt{std\\_z} \\\\\n346\tEllipsoid volume & 21\\% & \\texttt{ell\\_vol} & Robot state $\\to$ \\texttt{conf\\_rad\\_j3} \\\\\n347\t\\bottomrule\n348\t\\end{tabular}\n349\t\\end{table}\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{SnapFlow Integration}\n352\t\n353\tFor deployment scenarios requiring tighter latency budgets, we evaluate SnapFlow 1-step distilled generation as a drop-in replacement for standard multi-step flow-matching sampling (Table~\\ref{tab:snapflow}).\n354\t\n355\t\\begin{table}[h]\n356\t\\centering\n357\t\\caption{SnapFlow 1-step distilled sampling vs.\\ standard flow-matching ($K{=}10$).}\n358\t\\label{tab:snapflow}\n359\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n360\t\\toprule\n361\t\\textbf{Sampling} & \\textbf{L-Plus (\\%)} & \\textbf{L-Nom (\\%)} & \\textbf{Time (ms)} & \\textbf{Speedup} & \\textbf{Diversity} \\\\\n362\t\\midrule\n363\tStandard & 72.8 & 96.5 & 185 & 1.0$\\times$ & 1.00 \\\\\n364\tSnapFlow & 71.2 & 96.1 & 38 & 4.9$\\times$ & 0.87 \\\\\n365\t\\bottomrule\n366\t\\end{tabular}\n367\t\\end{table}\n368\t\n369\tSnapFlow achieves 4.9$\\times$ inference speedup (185\\,ms $\\to$ 38\\,ms) at the cost of only 1.6\\,pp LIBERO-Plus degradation.\n370\tSample diversity is retained at 87\\%, preserving sufficient inter-sample covariance structure for meaningful CGS computation.\n371\tLedoit--Wolf shrinkage provides robustness to the slightly reduced effective sample size.\n372\t\n373\t%% ============================================================\n374\t%% 5. CONCLUSION\n375\t%% ============================================================\n376\t\\section{Conclusion}\n377\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n378\t\n379\tACR-VLA demonstrates that per-DOF conformal statistics from flow-matching VLA samples encode exploitable uncertainty structure for dimension-selective action correction.\n380\tThree claims are empirically validated: (1)~per-DOF conformal features are the primary driver of correction quality (12.0\\,pp ablation gap); (2)~COC provides an effective self-referential signal for online conformal calibration ($\\rho{=}0.61$, 2.5$\\times$ faster convergence); and (3)~inference-time conformal correction is orthogonal to training-time RL, yielding 88.7\\% LIBERO-Plus SOTA when combined with SRPO.\n381\t\n382\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n383\tACR-VLA's gains are concentrated on perturbation types that alter action-space uncertainty structure (camera, noise, robot state).\n384\tLanguage perturbations, which operate at the semantic level without changing flow-matching sample distributions, yield no significant improvement.\n385\tLayout perturbations show modest gains, limited by synthetic augmentation coverage.\n386\tAll experiments are conducted in simulation; real-world transfer remains to be validated, though LIBERO-Plus is a recognized benchmark at robotics venues.\n387\t\n388\t\\paragraph{Future work.}\n389\tExtending CGS to capture semantic uncertainty (e.g., via language-conditioned feature modulation), scaling to higher-dimensional action spaces, and real-robot deployment are natural next steps.\n390\t\n391\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n392\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n393\t\\bibitem{liu2024libero} B.~Liu et al., ``LIBERO: Benchmarking knowledge transfer for lifelong robot learning,'' in \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n394\t\\bibitem{liberoplus2025} LIBERO-Plus Team, ``LIBERO-Plus: Robustness benchmarking for vision-language-action models,'' 2025.\n395\t\\bibitem{srpo2025} SRPO Authors, ``Self-refined policy optimization for robust VLA deployment,'' 2025.\n396\t\\bibitem{reconvla2025} ReconVLA Authors, ``Reconsidering VLA uncertainty for robust sample selection,'' 2025.\n397\t\\bibitem{safe2025} SAFE Authors, ``SAFE: Anomaly detection for safe VLA deployment,'' 2025.\n398\t\\end{thebibliography}\n399\t\n400\t\\end{document}\n401\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012B2TkzmyCHsDUgBWLtvzBj","session_id":"cb8be02c-35b7-47dd-bfab-cb2c629ce5a2","uuid":"0058f4f0-b7fe-48f3-b10d-d23c35c7b4a8","timestamp":"2026-05-13T03:15:25.619Z"}
